 people''s shoes."}],"answerId":"A","hint":"A short answer can begin with To. In a full sentence, place the purpose phrase after the main action. These tasks practise this to-infinitive pattern. Focus on **why answer** in this question.","explanation":"A purpose answer can stand alone: To catch mud from people''s shoes.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"why-answer","contextKey":"purpose-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-infinitive-of-purpose"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"41c290402a7bca2a57328f35040849aaac4520b99c202d2705b0c1efeab36886","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **why answer**. A short answer can begin with To. In a full sentence, place the purpose phrase after the main action. These tasks practise this to-infinitive pattern.","A purpose answer can stand alone: To catch mud from people''s shoes.","Choose **To catch mud from people''s shoes.**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A purpose answer can stand alone: To catch mud from people''s shoes.","B":"For does not form the requested **to + base verb** purpose construction here. A for + -ing phrase can describe an object''s function in a different pattern. A purpose answer can stand alone: To catch mud from people''s shoes.","C":"This option uses an **-ing form** where the purpose infinitive needs a **base verb after to**. A purpose answer can stand alone: To catch mud from people''s shoes.","D":"The purpose construction requires **to + base verb**. This option omits to, duplicates it or changes the following verb away from its base form. A purpose answer can stand alone: To catch mud from people''s shoes."}}},{"id":"50f18f84-3f0f-57fb-a7bb-635631461f19","topicSlug":"infinitive-of-purpose","level":"A2","prompt":"Combine this action and aim using a to-infinitive: “They put a mat by the door.” Aim: “catch mud from people''s shoes”.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"They put a mat by the door to catch mud from people''s shoes."},{"id":"B","text":"They put a mat by the door for catch mud from people''s shoes."},{"id":"C","text":"They put a mat by the door to catching mud from people''s shoes."},{"id":"D","text":"They put a mat by the door for to catch mud from people''s shoes."}],"answerId":"A","hint":"A short answer can begin with To. In a full sentence, place the purpose phrase after the main action. These tasks practise this to-infinitive pattern. Focus on **link action goal** in this question.","explanation":"Attach the goal as to catch mud from people''s shoes after the main action.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"link-action-goal","contextKey":"purpose-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-infinitive-of-purpose"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"001087eba5a1bcb76d0f5c369fb9d17e85458211a70f56fbaaff5b733985027e","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **link action goal**. A short answer can begin with To. In a full sentence, place the purpose phrase after the main action. These tasks practise this to-infinitive pattern.","Attach the goal as to catch mud from people''s shoes after the main action.","Choose **They put a mat by the door to catch mud from people''s shoes.**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Attach the goal as to catch mud from people''s shoes after the main action.","B":"The purpose construction requires **to + base verb**. This option omits to, duplicates it or changes the following verb away from its base form. Attach the goal as to catch mud from people''s shoes after the main action.","C":"This option uses an **-ing form** where the purpose infinitive needs a **base verb after to**. Attach the goal as to catch mud from people''s shoes after the main action.","D":"The purpose construction requires **to + base verb**. This option omits to, duplicates it or changes the following verb away from its base form. Attach the goal as to catch mud from people''s shoes after the main action."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["A purpose infinitive explains why someone does an action. It expresses a goal, not necessarily an achieved result. The basic pattern is a main action followed by to + base verb. Its understood subject normally matches the actor in the main clause."],"rules":[{"title":"Answering why","explanation":"A to-infinitive can express an action’s purpose: the goal somebody intended. It answers a why question.","examples":["We stopped to admire the view.","She called to confirm the address.","We saved money **to buy** a new fridge."],"pattern":"Main action + **to + base verb**","paragraphs":["In I called to ask a question, I both call and intend to ask. The to-infinitive supplies the purpose. The verb after to stays in base form regardless of the subject or the tense of the main action. I called to asked incorrectly marks the second verb as past."]},{"title":"Keep the base form","explanation":"Use to followed by the base verb, without an -ing ending. The subject of the main action can vary; the purpose verb stays in its base form.","examples":["They met to discuss the budget.","He met us to discuss the budget.","I spoke quietly **in order not to disturb** them."],"pattern":"**Why** did you call? — **To ask** a question.","paragraphs":["In order to and so as to can make purpose explicit. Negative purpose normally uses in order not to or so as not to. Ordinary not to is possible in some contexts, but a clear full pattern is useful for learners."]},{"title":"Link the action and its aim","explanation":"A short answer can begin with To. In a full sentence, place the purpose phrase after the main action. These tasks practise this to-infinitive pattern.","examples":["Why did you stop? To rest.","We stopped to rest.","She moved aside **so that I could pass**."],"pattern":"**in order not to** wake anyone","paragraphs":["If the intended actor changes, a so that clause is often clearer: I opened the door so that she could enter. The purpose can be placed first, often followed by a comma, when it frames the whole sentence."]},{"title":"Contrasts and edge cases","explanation":"For + -ing often describes an object’s function: a brush for cleaning bottles. It is not the normal substitute for a person’s purpose in I went to the shop to buy milk. A purpose does not prove success: she hurried to catch the bus but missed it is coherent.","examples":["We saved money **to buy** a new fridge.","I spoke quietly **in order not to disturb** them.","She moved aside **so that I could pass**."]}],"recap":["Main action + **to + base verb**","**Why** did you call? — **To ask** a question.","**in order not to** wake anyone"]},"stage":"4 · Connecting meanings","prerequisites":["past-habits"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
